														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4851428">
									

<p>Hello caspian2, thank you for responding! I appreciate the interest in the concept. After speaking in the IRC chat I discussed and most people said that due to the nature of this anomaly it would make sense if he developed it around 13~ when he went through his first breakup, however that wouldn't explain why the anomaly itself occured. I foolishly didn't preemptively think of how that'd be. I'll begin to brainstorm how to improve on the concept of how the anomaly was gained. I made a specific vow to not crosslink SCPs so I can't use another person's SCP, as I don't want to seem as if I'm riding off of their success - <strong>especially</strong> since this is my first SCP.</p>
<p>The reason they'd die the painful deaths is due to the curse/anomaly affected SCP-XXXX, which I still need to decide the cause of. I should be more specific and say that it's an extremely painful death - the pain dependent on how strong the mentioned relationship. Yes, acquaintance counts as a relationship. However it's only under verbal or written recognition where the effect appears. So if it simply isn't mentioned then it'll be fine.</p>
<p>With suicide patients, usually they can be sent to mental wards or the like to receive therapy. Inside of this therapy, usually catered towards people with extreme mental disorders, the main way which they're assisted by psychologists is via forming a strong, trusting relationship with them. However, he quite obviously can't do that.</p>
<p>I also decided during the time of discussion in the IRC chat that they actually turned themselves into the SCP foundation. I'd need to choose how they discovered the SCP Foundation, but that's the angle I want. They feel bad for wanting the attention so they try to trap themselves, but still get desperate and try to escape.</p>
<p>Due to Leveritas' advice, I've also decided to make a non-humanoid SCP first as that'd probably be a better way to start myself off. Thank you for the advice - I'll remember it for when I return to the concept later on in time.</p>

